'''valanche†''' (va-lanch′), ''n.'' [Also ''vollenge''; a dial. aphetic form of ''avalanche''.] An avalanche.

{{smaller block|The ''vollenge'' which overwhelms a whole village was at first but a little snow-ball.
{{right|''W. Taylor'', Survey of German Poetry, II. 456. (''Davies''.)}}

The great danger of travelling here when the sun is up proceeds from what they call the ''valanches''.
{{right|''Smollett'', France and Italy, xxxviii.}}}}

'''Valdenses, Valdensian.''' Same as ''Waldenses'', ''Waldensian''.

'''vale<sup>1</sup>''' (vāl), ''n.'' [< ME. ''vale'', ''val'', < OF. (and F.) ''val'' = Pr. ''val'', ''valh'' = Cat. ''vall'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''valle'', < L. ''vallis'', a vale; connections uncertain. Hence ult. ''valley'', ''avale'', ''avalanche'', ''vail''<sup>3</sup>.] '''1.''' A tract of low ground between hills; a valley: little used except in poetry. See ''valley''.

{{smaller block|<poem>And when thaire fase war thus for-done,
To the ''vale'' of ebron come thai sone.</poem>
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 73.}}
<poem>Along the cool sequestered ''vale'' of life
They kept the noiseless tenor of their way.</poem>
{{right|''Gray'', Elegy.}}

I pity people who weren’t born in a ''vale''. I don’t mean a flat country, but a ''vale''; that is, a flat country bounded by hills. ''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby, i. 1.}}

